{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788269219,"nonce":"CZCF8woNDCRnNlp+woNst7f3REI=","message":"THREE INVESTIGATION ITEMS RESOLVED against the shipped code and the decompiled\nsource, 2026-09-01, by the round that follows session 13. All three were raised\nas \"resolve by investigation, do not queue\" for this round. Two produce no work\nat all; one produces a spec, filed separately.\n\n=====================================================================\n1. DESIGNATOR COVERAGE — NOT A GAP. NOTHING TO FILE.\n=====================================================================\n\nThe question was \"is there deconstruct / cancel / build-roof? A build round\nneeds cancel and deconstruct at minimum.\" It was asked after looking in\n`DesignateEngine.cs`, which is the TARGET RESOLVER (rect/cells/things/filter),\nnot the designator table. The table is `DesignationVerbs.cs`, and it ships:\n\n    deconstruct, deconstruct-conduit, uninstall, cancel, claim, fill-in,\n    strip, open, eject-fuel, extract-skull, mine, mine-vein, chop,\n    harvest-wood, harvest, cut, cut-plants, extract-tree, haul, hunt,\n    slaughter, tame, release-to-wild, smooth, smooth-floor, smooth-wall,\n    remove-floor, remove-foundation\n\neach entry naming the `Designator_*` class whose own `CanDesignateCell` /\n`CanDesignateThing` is the gate the verb runs, and each gated additionally by\n`des.Visible` — the same `Gizmo.Visible` property this issue's amendment #1\nmakes the widget-gate argument about.\n\n`build-roof` / `no-roof` / `ignore-roof` are NOT missing either; they are in\n`AreaVerbs.cs` under the `area` verb, which is where the game itself puts them\n(`Designator_AreaBuildRoof` / `_AreaNoRoof` / `_AreaIgnoreRoof` are area\ndesignators, not cell designators). `AreaVerbs` already documents the\n`Designator_AreaNoRoof.justAddedCells` static-list hazard that makes `Finalize`\nmandatory there.\n\nBEARING ON THIS SPEC, and it is a simplification: `cancel` is\n`Designator_Cancel`, whose `DesignateThing` \"removes every cancelable\ndesignation on the target and destroys player blueprints/frames there\" — the\ntable's own note. So **`cancel-layout` is placement-id bookkeeping over a\nshipped verb**, not new game-facing work. Its job is to remember which\nblueprints a placement id owns and to point the existing designator at them.\n\n=====================================================================\n2. ROOFING — NO SPEC. ONE LINE OWED TO THIS ISSUE'S ACCEPTANCE.\n=====================================================================\n\n\"A room is not a room without one\" is true and already served three ways:\n\n- **Designation**: `area {kind:\"build-roof\"|\"no-roof\"|\"ignore-roof\", ...}`,\n  shipped, gated on each designator's own `CanDesignateCell`.\n- **Automatic**: amendment #2 and its verification are both correct —\n  `AutoBuildRoofAreaSetter.TryGenerateAreaNow` roofs any enclosed,\n  non-map-edge, non-fogged player room of \u003c=26 regions and \u003c=320 cells. A 7x7\n  module is 49 cells and a 5x7 rehearsal is 35; both roof themselves.\n- **Observation**: `map-dump` reads `map.roofGrid.RoofAt(c)` (and knows\n  `isThickRoof`), `map-view` carries a `roof` layer in its priority, and\n  `find-rect` takes `require:[\"roofed\"]` / `[\"unroofed\"]`.\n\nWhat is owed is not a verb but a TEST DISCIPLINE, and it comes straight from\nthis issue's own verification comment #4: `TryGenerateAreaFor` only QUEUES\n(`queuedGenerateRooms.Add(room)`); `TryGenerateAreaNow` runs from\n`AutoBuildRoofAreaSetterTick_First`, i.e. NEXT TICK. **An acceptance that reads\nthe roof area in the same call as the placement sees nothing and reports a\ncorrect implementation as broken.** Advance one tick first. Recorded here\nbecause it is the kind of thing that costs a session a day and looks like a mod\nbug.\n\n=====================================================================\n3. \"DOES THE WORK HAPPEN?\" — THE ONE REAL GAP, AND IT IS NOT ABOUT WORK\n=====================================================================\n\nThe question was framed as work priorities and hauling: does a colonist with\nConstruction enabled actually build the blueprint, given M1 showed\n`Alert_ColonistsIdle` for the whole run.\n\nThe work-setting half is shipped. `work-priorities` exists (`PawnManageVerbs`),\nincluding `manual:true`, `copy_from` and the `Notify_UseWorkPrioritiesChanged`\nfan-out that a raw `SetPriority` loop would skip.\n\n**The gap is observation, and it is total:** `grep Blueprint\nSource/AutoRimmer/` returns ZERO hits. Nothing in the mod reads a `Blueprint`,\na `Frame`, `Frame.WorkLeft`, `Frame.resourceContainer` or\n`Blueprint.TotalMaterialCost`. `digest` has no construction section at all —\nits sections are time, site, alerts, colonists, resources, power, threats,\nchanged.\n\nSo the honest statement of the M1 failure is not \"the colonists were idle\". It\nis that **`build` could place a blueprint and no verb could tell you whether it\nwas stalled for materials, being worked, or finished** — which is exactly the\nshape of M1's invisible research bench, one level up. Idleness was the symptom\nthe agent could see; the blueprint's own state was the thing it could not.\n\nFiled as its own spec with an Acceptance section rather than absorbed into this\none, because it is a READ and this issue is a WRITE, and because 4.2's play loop\nneeds it whether or not `place-layout` ever lands.","files":null}]}